Q: Reading string as a stream without copying A: <p><strong>If you are using .NET 11, you can opt in for <code>StringStream</code> provided in the runtime:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/core/whats-new/dotnet-11/libraries#in-memory-stream-adapters" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/core/whats-new/dotnet-11/libraries#in-memory-stream-adapters</a></li>
</ul>
<p>You could use a combination <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.io.unmanagedmemorystream" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>UnmanagedMemoryStream</code></a> &amp; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.encoding.createtranscodingstream" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Encoding.CreateTranscodingStream</code></a> but that does require <strong><code>unsafe</code></strong> code to be used.</p>
<p>We basically:</p>
<ul>
<li>Represent the string as a <code>byte*</code> via unsafe code.</li>
<li>Wrap the pointer as a stream using <code>UnmanagedMemoryStream</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The provided class provided an implementation of a <code>ReadOnlyStringStream</code>.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>Memory&lt;T&gt;</code> can be used instead of <code>GCHandle</code> for newer .NET versions.</li>
<li>The string must be pinned using <code>GCHandle</code> or <code>Memory&lt;T&gt;</code> instead of <code>fixed</code>.</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public sealed class ReadOnlyStringStream : UnmanagedMemoryStream
{
    readonly GCHandle _handle;

    public ReadOnlyStringStream(string value)
    {
        _handle = GCHandle.Alloc(value, GCHandleType.Pinned);

        var pointer = _handle.AddrOfPinnedObject();
        var length = sizeof(char) * value.Length;

        unsafe
        {
            Initialize((byte*)pointer, length, length, FileAccess.Read);
        }
    }

    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing)
    {
        if (_handle.IsAllocated)
        {
            _handle.Free();
        }
        base.Dispose(disposing);
    }

    ~ReadOnlyStringStream() =&gt; Dispose(false);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now you can use it as follows as shown here:</p>
<ul>
<li>Use <code>ReadOnlyStringStream</code> to make a stream for a <code>string</code>.</li>
<li>Use <code>Encoding.CreateTranscodingStream</code> to encode the stream using UTF8.
<ul>
<li>NET strings are UTF-16 or <code>Encoding.Unicode</code> encoded.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Copy the encoded stream to a <code>MemoryStream</code> and print the output.</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>
var value = &quot;Hello World!&quot;;

using ReadOnlyStringStream stringStream = new(value);
using var encodingStream = Encoding.CreateTranscodingStream(stringStream, Encoding.Unicode, Encoding.UTF8, true);

using MemoryStream memoryStream = new();
encodingStream.CopyTo(memoryStream);

Console.WriteLine(Encoding.UTF8.GetString(memoryStream.ToArray()));
</code></pre>
